/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 20 — The Stair's Floor
     2	
     3	Teague's crew held the rim in the morning without anybody asking them to, and he catalogued that from the stone before he catalogued anything else, because it was the first thing on the ground that had not been there the day before.
     4	
     5	Four of them, at the four quarters of the bowl, on the rim's lip where the hooded six had stood — the tall woman with the spear on the south, above the road's far cut; the broad man with the crossbow and the limp on the east; the young one on the north, above the stone; Teague on the west, where the last of the six had stopped and looked back. They had walked up in the dark and taken the rim as a crew takes a job, and they had not come down to the road to say so, and they were not going to. Teague had said *if you're late back, we'll come look*, and had come, and had looked, and the looking had turned into holding, and a captain who holds a rim for a crew ten lines under him does not want it entered anywhere but the ground.
     6	
     7	"He's outside the line," Karis said, beside him, with the lamp already lit and the sun not yet on the floor. "All four. He's read the stone."
     8	
     9	"He's read the stone," Cael said, and they went down.
    10	
    11	---
    12	
    13	Six over, in the order Seln gave, and Oryn checked her hands at the stride and said "Gone," and Brom crossed with the box on his good shoulder and the bad one carried the way a man carries a thing that has been mended and told not to be used: still, and slightly forward, and not mentioned. Nobody asked him to leave the box. Nobody was going to.
    14	
    15	Cael crossed with nothing in his hands and the rim's bill still in his legs. The suite had gone out of him on a lip the evening before to the last framework, and it rang faint this morning, as it did on second mornings, all ten with a hollow in each, and his legs had stopped having opinions and had not yet started having the other thing; he had slept in a rut with a stone at his back and had eaten what Brom put in his hand, and the unit had looked at him at the stone and decided, without a word, that he would carry no box today, and he had let them decide it, because a man who has spent everything on a rim owes the people who watched him do it the courtesy of being seen to be spent.
    16	
    17	The bowl in the morning was the bowl of the morning before, and it was not, because the six of them walked across it under four people who were theirs and not six who were not, and the difference was in the scrub, which was only scrub, and in the floor, which took the first of the sun and went white along its whole width while they were still on the road's cut, and in the hole at the floor's middle, which was a hole and not a door somebody had been waiting for them to run to.
    18	
    19	Teague said one thing, from the west rim, before they went down, in the voice a captain uses across a bowl: "We'll hold it till you're up." It was not an offer. It was the shape of the day, stated once. Cael said "Noted," up the slope, and Teague turned his shoulder to the bowl the way he turned it to a fire, and that was the whole of the conversation the two crews had that day, and it was, Cael thought, going down, exactly as much as either of them wanted.
    20	
    21	They went down the forty steps with two lamps.
    22	
    23	The air changed at the twentieth step. He had not marked it the day before, with a bolt-wound man ahead of him and six crossbows behind; he marked it now: cold, still, dry, with nothing in it — no earth, no water, no root, none of the smells a hole in the ground has because holes in the ground are made of ground — and the two lamps burned in it straight up, without a flicker, as if the chamber had never once had a draught in it, which, he thought, catalogued as a fact and not a reason, it had not.
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	He catalogued the chamber by lamp, properly, because the day before he had catalogued it in the length of a breath with a bolt-wound man beside him, and a count taken in a breath is a count.
    28	
    29	Square, or near it: ten strides by ten, walked. Fused stone, floor and walls and ceiling, and the ceiling was the floor — the underside of the plate the six of them had run across in the morning and the bowl's rim had watched all afternoon — one piece of grey from the stair's foot to the far wall, a hand above Brom's head, no join in it anywhere, so that the chamber was not a room under a floor but the floor's *other side*, and he stood under it and put his palm flat on the ceiling, a man touching a ceiling, with nothing running in him but the hollow the rim had left — and it was one piece to the hand as it was to the eye, cold, dry, no join under his palm anywhere, and he did not write the word for that either.
    30	
