editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:Six people stood at the stone. The sun was coming up behind them over the ridge, and their shadows went before them down the old road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:13:She said it quite quietly, at the stride. She had crossed such an edge more often than any of them, and she had told Cael at the fire that she did not expect ever to grow used to it; but she did not break her pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:17:Brom came last. Cael heard him grunt at the stride, as he had grunted on the Fallow, like a man who has felt a door shut behind him. The healer's bow was on his left shoulder, spanned, with a bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:41:Something was wrong with that shot, and though he had only fifteen yards to cover, it took him the whole of them to say what. A bow on that brow could have let all five of them walk down to the floor, and had them two hundred yards inside, with nothing over them and nowhere to go. It had chosen to shoot the last man instead, when he was five strides from the way out, with a healer standing in plain sight at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:47:At twelve feet there was a greyness in the air between the bars, like breath. He took one stride more, and it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:61:The healer did not so much as turn her head. She had Brom's left arm across her shoulders and she was bringing him up the road toward the stone at a stumbling run, with her eyes on the grey wool of his coat where a dark stain was beginning. She had three strides to go, and Cael saw her thinking of nothing in the world but those three strides, at the end of which she would be a Tide again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:She did not wait to be answered. She stepped in close under Brom's good arm, and with her right hand she lifted the healer's bow off his left shoulder by its stock, spanned as it was, with the bolt held in the groove under her thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:Karis was away first, with her coat held up in both fists. Brom ran. A bolt in the shoulder is not a bolt in the leg, and he went down that road like a bullock, with the feathers of the bolt nodding at his shoulder and the healer at his left hand, matching him stride for stride with her fingers hooked in his belt. Seln ran a yard to his right. Cael came last, since somebody must, and since if any of them fell he wished to be the one who saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:He was on the floor himself then. It was as cold through his boots as the other had been, and as silent, and thirty yards of it had never seemed so far. The hole was in front of him. It was two strides square, with an edge as sharp as a box's; and on its near side a step went down, and another, and a third, each of them white with frost and sunlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:193:It was square, and Cael put it at ten strides each way. The floor was the same grey, level and seamless. So was the roof. He looked up, and it took him a breath to understand what he was looking at: the roof of that room was the underside of the floor that they had run across. There was no beam in it and no arch; it lay over them like a lid, and he judged it a foot above Brom's head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:227:"I've one in the groove," she said. "There's four more in the sleeve on his belt, and I can't span it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:267:"It's high. It's missed the lung and it's missed the big vessel, or he'd not be talking. Leave it in, because the bolt's the plug. When my hands work again I'll take it out and close what it opened. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:13:"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:31:It was strange beyond telling to stand so. She had stopped a stride and a half from every person that he had ever seen her meet, until her hands were wanted; and he had been read by her six times and had never once touched her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:159:They brought the mules down to the brow. Karis gave Brom her box-rope to hold, and went on by herself down the old road toward the floor, with her arms a little out from her sides, counting under her breath. Cael walked a stride behind her and to one side, and he had no need to count, since he was watching the back of her neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:161:At forty paces she stopped as if she had come to a wall. Her sigil had gone out at that stride, as it had gone out on the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:163:She stood for one breath. Then she stepped backward, a single stride, and let her shoulders down; and she bent, and picked a stone out of the old rut, grey and as big as a loaf, and set it on the turf at her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:167:One by one they came down and tried it, since no one of them would take another's word for such a thing: Lira, and Brom, and Seln, each a stride past the stone and a stride back. The healer went over last, with two fingers on her own wrist and her lips moving, and came out again and nodded. Cael walked across beside Brom, and felt what he had felt on the Fallow, which was grass under his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:199:"That first has stood five cold terms. I said so of its fellow on the Fallow, and I see no cause to alter it." He bent closer. "This has had one. Two, at the most. And it is the same number. Thirty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:225:She came by Cael afterward, at her stride and a half, with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:3:He let Shadow go while he lay in the ditch, with his arm still round a bar of the registry's iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:83:The man took one step backward. He took another. He did not turn his hood away from the flame until he was five yards off; and Cael did not follow him by a single stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:119:The healer came over between two of them, with Brom's good arm across her shoulders and his weight on her; and Cael watched her do what he had seen her do at a rock on the Fallow, eight times in one grey morning, when she thought that nobody saw. She took one stride past the stone. She stopped dead, with her eyes shut and her lips moving, and her free hand closed on Brom's wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:161:The healer looked at the palm from her stride and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:317:He gave it one hour more by the shadow of the wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:325:Not one of the three asked him why. The spear took his spear. The bow got up off the bank, on the good leg first, and slung his stave; and Teague, who had been about to tell him that he might stay with the mill and the mules, saw his face and held his tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:45:He owed a healer two readings of himself with the tenth fragment, on two different days, with a night between; and after those he owed her the whole of it at once. She had set it at a well that morning, with a pannier on the coping. Nine days had gone by since the first reading, in a loft, and he had let every one of them pass. She had seen why from a stride and a half. He had found a place in himself that did not move, and he had been afraid of it, and he had liked it better as a line on a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:195:"Nothing else ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:197:"I'd not take it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:55:"At what? There's nobody in it." She did not turn her head. "I've one in the groove. If I loose it at a patch of sky I've an empty bow that I can't span, and Seln has to come up six steps into the light to do it for me. He'd like that, up there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:87:The cold was the worst of it. There was no frost in that room, and no wind; but the stone drank the warmth out of whatever touched it, as dry ground drinks water. Cael had given his coat to Brom, and by the middle of the white he could not feel his feet. He walked the south wall with his fingers on it, ten strides and back, and at the fourth turn his hand met wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:191:The healer had got to her feet; he could hear it, and he knew where she had stopped: a stride and a half from him, in the pitch dark, as she stopped everywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:219:It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:239:He had been waiting for it. A thing made for hiding costs from the moment that a person wakes; he had paid that on every day since it came to him, by carrying it shut. To have it open was a different bill. It began in the ends of his fingers, as it had begun in a lane on the Fallow, and it was not the frost, since it came from inside. At fifty strides he could not feel his nails; at a hundred his hands hung at the ends of his arms like things that he was carrying for somebody, and the cold had gone into his wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:277:The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:57:She let that stand by itself for a breath, so that nobody should mistake it for the start of something longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:109:"Take it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:111:"They'll send people. A lure's no use if nobody's waiting at the end of it. People have faces. Six years I've wanted a face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:195:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:He had not heard him cross the room, though the man's three were on their feet behind him, at their distances, and it was the first time that Cael had stood within a stride of him since a river bank. He was no taller than Cael. He had grey over the ears and a hand-axe in his belt with its edge forward; and he kept his eyes on Cael's face and off his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:63:She was an old woman, very small and very upright, with two grandsons to carry her and a stick laid along her side that she plainly meant to walk home on. Her left knee was the size of a loaf under her skirt. The healer looked at it from her stride and a half, and then at its owner, and something came into her tired face that Cael had seen there only once before, at a board with a goat on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:79:That one took her until the shadow of the wheel lay across the door, and when it was done she sat on her keg with her head down between her shoulders for as long as it takes to count fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:93:She was speaking to him. She had washed to the elbow in a bucket, and she stood drying her hands on her breeches, a stride and a half inside the lean-to, with the old woman's hurdle going away up the valley behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:95:He got up off the bench and went to the edge of the turf roof's shadow, and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:37:It took Lira the first three cords to learn the trick of it, and after that she did not put a foot wrong. Cael walked behind with the notebook, and wrote each station in a bad hand. She went with the cord's end shut in her right fist and the sling tied high against her collar-bone, a pace at a time, as a child walks along a kerb; and every few strides she tipped her head, as if she were listening, and moved her left boot a hand's breadth out or in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:53:"It is the same," she said. "Cord for cord, and the odd strides are the same number of strides." Her voice was not quite steady. "I said two hundred, from the ridge, by my eye, and I was twenty short of it, and a stride. It is the Fallow's round, and so it is the Fallow's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:57:"To a stride. I should like somebody to tell me how that comes about by accident, five days' walk apart." She shut the book on her finger. "Nobody will. I am going down to look at a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:87:Cael paced the room from the door to the western wall, and then across. It was ten strides each way, as he had put it the day before, and that much of his count held. The roof did not. He had called it a foot above Brom's head; and Brom put up his left hand, and his palm lay on the grey with the elbow still bent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:The healer had stopped at her stride and a half from the nearest wall. She had been round eleven stones on the Fallow thirty times and had never gone near enough to see that they were cut, and she had sat with her back to this for a whole day in the dark. She held up her two palms to it, slowly, as she held them to a patient whom she had not yet touched; and then she let them fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Then this is the book of whoever drove them." Seln kept his eyes on the leaf. "I put five cold terms on the old iron at the stone, and I see no cause to alter that for his book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"It has. So that mark lay in this man's case five cold terms ago, and somebody set the same on a bar at our stone a good deal later. Those are two visits, and I can tell you no more of them. I said that I should not put a meaning on two strokes of a punch; I shall not put a date on them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:283:"Forty-one," Karis said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:287:"Kinds. Forty-one on the Fallow, when the doubtful ones are taken out or let in as this wall bids, and forty-one in the courses here. I have been over the courses of these four walls for six hours, looking for a forty-second, and there is none in them; and there is not one of the forty-one that is wanting." She looked up. "A man with apple trees told me that I wanted to see them both at the thaw. I have seen them both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*

