"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "I'd go back to it better if I had felt one. So I'm asking whether I may read you, here, when the bench is done. The short one, and no more than you choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:117 count=1
He stood for as long as it took him to be sure that he had nothing to add, and he had nothing. He had asked a person for the first time in his life whether he might do a thing to her; she had said that he might not; and the whole of what was left to him was to go and sit down. He went and sat down. It was not hard to do, and he was a little surprised at that, since he had dreaded it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:123 count=1
*Hundred and thirteenth day. Millrace, the healer's bench, a little after noon. Asked her, at her own bench, whether I might read her. It is the first time that I have asked anybody. She said no. Entered before anything else today, since it is the first of its kind and I want it to head the leaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:19 count=1
"At that bench you asked the healer, at her own table, with three mendings just gone out of her and eleven people looking on. Nobody is read at that table but by me; you turned it round on me once, and I told you what that was." She pushed back her left cuff. "And you'd have had your hand on a tired woman who was being stared at, and gone off thinking that was a Tide. This is a woman on a rock, who has eaten, and walked an hour, and there's nobody here who hasn't seen her asleep. She's the one you've asked, and she can say yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:251 count=1
Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.
