23:Karis had the long cord out by the time he came down.
25:It was the same that she had made at Thornwater, two box-ropes spliced end to end and marked off with twists of red wool; and she had it laid along the turf from the grey stone northward, with its near end under her boot. She looked as though she had not slept, and she looked very happy.
31:"You have one arm, and a hand at the end of it, and a sigil, and I need exactly those three. You take the far end, and walk out along the edge with your boot half on it, until the cord is taut. Where you stop, he cuts."
37:It took Lira the first three cords to learn the trick of it, and after that she did not put a foot wrong. Cael walked behind with the notebook, and wrote each station in a bad hand. She went with the cord's end shut in her right fist and the sling tied high against her collar-bone, a pace at a time, as a child walks along a kerb; and every few strides she tipped her head, as if she were listening, and moved her left boot a hand's breadth out or in.
41:At each cord's end Seln knelt and cut a turf, and Karis came up along the rope, counting, with her short cord in her other hand.
53:"It is the same," she said. "Cord for cord, and the odd strides are the same number of strides." Her voice was not quite steady. "I said two hundred, from the ridge, by my eye, and I was twenty short of it, and a stride. It is the Fallow's round, and so it is the Fallow's radius."
127:It was the cord that Cael's eye went along first, since it was the only thing there that was going anywhere. It ran out over the floor toward the eastern wall, pale and thin, with a knot in it at every fifth span, and it lay as straight as a ruled line for half the width of the room. There it stopped. The rest of its length lay beside it in a coil that nobody had paid out.
133:"It is a kit for one thing," said Karis. She had come away from her wall at last. "A cord to find how far, and a bar to mark where; a mallet for the bar, and a book to say that it was done. He did not come to map this country. He came to put iron on an edge."
137:"They lie in the order that they came out," said Seln quietly. "The cord, and the mallet, and the bar; and the book last and nearest, since a man writes last. Nobody packs so. He meant to take them up again."
195:"Then there are distances, measured in cords, as she measures; his cord is that one, knotted at the fives. He begins at the edge. *North station to floor.* *East station to floor.* There are eight of those, by the points of the compass and the half-points, and against every one of them he has put the same figure."
251:Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.
295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
305:"You are setting your cord beside his, and his figure is in a registry book. I have spent fifteen years learning what that office's paper is worth. I should not care to see you rest a finding on a number of theirs."
311:"I paced that round this morning with two box-ropes and a girl's left foot. He paced it with a knotted line, for people whom I should not trust to tell me the hour, and his line found what mine did." She laid her hand on the short cord. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it."
317:He had made up his mind to it while the others were rolling sheets. Seln had laid the mallet in the case, and the bar beside it, and the cord in its coil, and let down the lid; the book he had put inside his own coat, against the strap of the other thing that he carried, and nobody had questioned it. The case lay on the floor with its strap trailing.

