+   new: `*Hundred and thirteenth day. Millrace, the healer's bench, a little after noon. Asked her leave to touch the boy's wrist while she held it, so that I could learn what came to her hand. She said no. I stepped back. Entered before anything else today, since it is the first refusal under the rule and I want it to head the leaf.*`
+
+4. `manuscript/chapter-48.md`
+   old: `"At that bench you asked the healer, at her own table, with three mendings just gone out of her and eleven people looking on. Nobody is read at that table but by me; you turned it round on me once, and I told you what that was." She pushed back her left cuff. "And you'd have had your hand on a tired woman who was being stared at, and gone off thinking that was a Tide. This is a woman on a rock, who has eaten, and walked an hour, and there's nobody here who hasn't seen her asleep. She's the one you've asked, and she can say yes."`
+   new: `"At that bench you asked to touch a boy while I had his wrist, so that you could feel what I felt. His leave was never yours to ask from me." She pushed back her left cuff. "Here you've asked to read me. This is my wrist, and I can answer for it."`
+
+5. `manuscript/chapter-52.md`
+   old: `Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.`
+   new: `Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting the divisions within one line against the cord's scale rather than the distance along the stone, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.`

