+### A. Three tracked passages in chapter 52 — RESOLVED
+
+1. **The stair's air — RESOLVED.** The baseline chain began, “At the twentieth the air changed … both flames stood up straight and still,” then immediately supplied the cold and the missing smells. The repair enters on the bolt at the thirty-first step (`chapter-52.md:61–65`); Brom notices the wrong flame first—“It's stood like that since a good way back. Like a nail”—and only afterward does Seln locate the change: “Since the twentieth … The air stops there” (`52:69–79`). Oryn supplies the cold and absent earth/water smells after all six enter the chamber (`52:81–85`). The source's same-content/same-order chain is broken.
+2. **The “proper catalogue” — RESOLVED.** The diff deletes “Cael catalogued the room properly, since nobody had been able to the day before.” The repaired scene begins the chamber work with an actual check: “Cael paced the room from the door to the western wall, and then across” (`52:87`). The present measurement corrects yesterday's rushed roof estimate before the narration explains why all of them had misjudged it (`52:87–93`). The source frame—announced proper catalogue followed by a comparison with yesterday—is gone.
+3. **The open kit — RESOLVED.** The baseline opened, “It was a case … The lid stood open and thrown right back,” then moved to contents, taking-out order and interrupted work. The repair begins on the half-run cord—“It was the cord that Cael's eye went along first”—then lets Brom name the tools and Karis state their functions (`52:127–133`). The open case is identified only afterward (`52:135`), and Seln reads the taking-out order last (`52:137`). The required facts remain in a new order.
+
+I reopened source chapters 19–20 named in `source-tracking.md` and applied the same-content/same-order standard rather than relying on the automatic percentage. All three returned passages are **RESOLVED**. **New tracking: none.** The repaired roof correction, two-visit dating, and wall-work support do not reproduce a new source chain. The overlap command independently reports zero unprotected shared runs of eight or more words.
